\ Cross-worker bounded-memory regression for scope-exit reaping.
\
\ Repeatedly enter a short-lived nested task-scope that spawns four tracked children. `spawn` picks
\ the least-loaded worker and increments its load at dispatch, so four rapid spawns spread across
\ the four workers pinned by the `.flags` sidecar. Most children therefore run on a worker other
\ than the one exiting the scope.
\
\ A remote child can't be freed by the exiting worker without violating the owning-worker invariant.
\ Instead the exiting worker routes each remote child to its home worker's reap queue, and the home
\ worker frees it on its own thread.
\
\ Same-worker reaping alone leaves every remote child retained in its home worker's finished_tasks
\ until scheduler teardown. A regression that drops the reap-queue routing exhausts the 8 MB cap
\ set in the `.flags` sidecar partway through and dies with a memory-limit error instead of
\ printing the marker.
\
\ A nested task-scope blocks until every child is terminal, so no explicit yield is needed for the
\ children to finish before the scope drains and reaps them.
\
\ Bounded completion proves no leak. Clean termination proves no deadlock. Running the real binary
\ surfaces any use-after-free or stale diagnostic pointer.
\
\ The iteration count is sized empirically: with the routing dropped, the cap trips between 500 and
\ 750 iterations, so 1500 fails reliably while running about 1.2 seconds standalone against the
\ 10-second harness cap. That margin survives a fully loaded parallel suite run.

[
  1500 [
    [
      [ 1 1 + drop ] spawn drop
      [ 2 2 + drop ] spawn drop
      [ 3 3 + drop ] spawn drop
      [ 4 4 + drop ] spawn drop
    ] task-scope
  ] times
] task-scope

"bounded" print-line
